const CHelpBlocks: array[0..223] of record ID, English: AnsiString; end = (
  (ID: 'help.body.intro.title'; English: '{rIntroduction}'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.intro.roguelike'; English: '{rDRL} (abbr. for D, the Roguelike) is, as the name implies, a'#10'roguelike game. If you don''t know what a roguelike is... checkout '#10'wikipedia -- {Bhttp://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roguelike}.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.intro.premise'; English: 'DRL is inspired by a famous 1993 FPS hellish shooter. You''re'#10'the sole surviving marine from a squad sent to investigate a distress call'#10'from the moonbase of Phobos.  It turns out that all hell has broken loose'#10'on the plant... literally.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.intro.mission'; English: 'Your quest in DRL is to descend into the moonbase complex, find the'#10'source of this evil outbreak, and destroy it at all costs.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.intro.accessible-design'; English: 'Unlike many other roguelikes, DRL is intended to be simple and easy to'#10'get into. To that end, there is a limited inventory, maps of the size of'#10'one screen, and one item per tile. The number of keys you need to '#10'remember is also quite small compared to many other games in its genre.'),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.title'; English: '{rGetting Started}'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.menu-introduction'; English: 'When you first start a game of DRL, you''re presented with a menu screen'#10'with a few different options:'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.menu-options'; English: '{YNew game} will, well, start a new game!'#10'{YChallenge game} is initially locked, and becomes unlocked after you reach'#10'Corporal rank (need to survive 10 levels for it). Challenge games force you'#10'to play the game a certain way (e.g. using only melee), and you can raise'#10'your player rank in DRL by completing them. As your rank goes higher, '#10'more challenges will be made available to you.'#10'{YCustom game} brings up a selection screen where you can view and load'#10'modules. Modules are player-created levels, episodes, or entirely new games'#10'that bring a completely new experience to the DRL engine.'#10'{YShow highscores} displays the highscore list, not surprisingly.'#10'{YShow player} displays your player statistics, which include your player '#10'rank, number of kills, and number of wins. It has several screens which'#10'can be scrolled with the arrow keys.'#10'{YHelp} displays the help screen, which contains the info you are'#10'currently reading.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.start-first-game'; English: 'For now, select {YNew game} with the arrow keys and press ENTER to start'#10'your very first game of DRL!'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.difficulty'; English: 'Before you start your game, you have to make a few decisions. The first is'#10'the difficulty level. At the beginning, only three difficulty levels are'#10'available: "I''m too young to die", "Hey, Not Too Rough", and "Hurt Me'#10'Plenty". At higher difficulties, more monsters appear and tougher monsters'#10'appear earlier. "Ultra-Violence" and "Nightmare!" difficulties are unlocked'#10'as your player rank rises. At Nightmare! difficulty, only the most insane'#10'DRL warriors will stand a chance!'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.class'; English: 'The second decision is choosing a character class. Classes differ by '#10'starting perks, availability of one advanced trait as basic, and each of'#10'them has a different set of Master Traits.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.character-name'; English: 'The third decision you have to make is what your name will be in the game.'#10'You can figure that much on your own, right? You can also leave it blank'#10'and wear the name of a random legendary warrior...'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.starting-trait'; English: 'The fourth and final choice to make is select a starting "trait". '#10'Traits are upgrades or "perks" that you earn when you gain levels in '#10'DRL (by killing monsters). Traits can raise your max health, let you'#10'run faster, deal more damage per shot, or otherwise make you more awesome.'#10'Some traits can be levelled up individually.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.advanced-master-traits'; English: 'You''ll notice a few traits aren''t available. Some are "Advanced Traits",'#10'and require you to learn a normal trait first. Advanced Traits are more'#10'situational but quite effective, too. The rest are master traits, which'#10'require you to take a specific set of traits (and block off a few other'#10'traits) and give you some sort of great unique bonus. But hey, don''t think'#10'about it too hard. Just pick one you like and move on.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.screen-introduction'; English: 'And that''s it, you''re ready to play the game! After a brief intro, you will'#10'be greeted with something like this (ASCII map is present if playing in'#10'ASCII mode):'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.screen-example'; English: '{d---------------------------------------------------------------------------}'#10' Welcome to DRL...'#10' ##########'#10' #@.........'#10' #..........'#10' #.........'#10' #.........'#10' #........'#10' #........'#10' #.......'#10' #......'#10' #....'#10' #..'#10#10#10#10#10#10#10#10#10#10' {bDRLguy}{d                     Armor : }{lnone}'#10' {dHealth:}{r 100%}{d Exp:}{l  1}{d/}{l0%}{l    Weapon: }{lpistol (2d4) [6/6]}'#10' cautious                                                  {rPhobos Base Entry}'#10'{d---------------------------------------------------------------------------}'#10'                             {bThe Starting Screen}'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.first-floor'; English: 'This is a map of the first level of the moonbase on Phobos. The whole level'#10'is exactly the size of the screen. Walk around (using the arrow keys or'#10'numpad) and get used to the interface. Don''t get yourself killed!'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.message-area'; English: 'Take a look at the screen. It is divided into three parts. On top you have'#10'the "message area", made of two lines of text. Watch this space carefully'#10'-- it may notify you of impending danger or tell you that you''re out of'#10'ammo (poor sob!).'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.health-experience'; English: 'On the bottom you have the status bar. The top line displays your name, and'#10'below it is the most important stat -- your health. If this one drops'#10'below one percent, you''re dead, dude - nothing can save you. Scanning '#10'to the right you can see the experience indicator. The first number is your'#10'character level -- the higher it is, the tougher you are. The second number'#10'is your experience displayed as a percentage. Each character level requires'#10'a certain experience amount needed to achieve it.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.tactics'; English: 'Below your health and experience is "tactics". By default your tactic is'#10'"cautious". This tactic gives no benefits or disadvantages. If you press'#10'X once, it will change to "running" (note that changing tactics takes'#10'some in-game time). With this tactic you have increased movement speed,'#10'dodge chance, and defense bonus, but have a smaller chance to hit and'#10'reduced melee damage. After some time spent running (or after pressing'#10'X again), you will get "tired". Being tired has no effects, positive'#10'or negative (like cautious), but while tired, you can''t run again. You can'#10'return to the cautious tactic by leveling up, gaining health by most'#10'methods, or descending to next level. Since running is limited in this way,'#10'try not to waste it. At the same time, however, remember to use it, as they'#10'don''t stockpile for later levels.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.equipment-status'; English: 'In the column to the right of that, you have two indicators, "Armor" and'#10'"Weapon", that show you what armor and weapon you have equipped. For'#10'weapons, the number in brackets indicates the damage that the weapon'#10'deals, and the two numbers in square brackets indicate the current-loaded'#10'ammo and clip size for that weapon (e.g., [4/6] means you have four'#10'bullets in a magazine that can hold six).'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.map-symbols'; English: 'Between the status bar and message bar, there''s the map. The "@" sign with'#10'the cursor below it -- that''s you (aren''t you ugly?). See any letters'#10'nearby? It may be a "T"ree, but chances are it''s the enemy. Kill''em all.'#10'The "#" are walls, "+" is a closed door, "/" an open door. Dots are normal'#10'floor. Red dots are either Phobos''s ugly rock landscape (on the first'#10'level), or blood (anywhere else).'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.inventory-equipment'; English: 'Press "i" to see your inventory. It''s pretty empty, right? Your character'#10'starts equipped with a few things -- your trusty pistol (already equipped),'#10'a handful of ammo, and a couple of med-packs. To check equipped item,'#10'press "e" in-game to see the full list (which includes boots and a second'#10'weapon slot).'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.pickup-consumables'; English: 'Your pistol and ammo are not going to last you very long, though.  Press'#10'"g" to pick up new weapons (represented by "}") and ammo ("|").  If you'#10'walk around that first level you''re likely to run into some small medkits'#10'(red "+") that you can pick up and add to your inventory. Later on you'#10'might find other consumable items, they''re all "+"s. Press "i" at any time'#10'and a consumable to use it at any time. If you ever want to drop something'#10'(usually to make room for new weapons or items), press "Backspace" in the'#10'inventory screen to drop items.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.levers-barrels'; English: 'Along the way, you''re bound to run into levers ("&") and barrels ("0"). To'#10'pull a lever, stand on it and press "SPACE" to use them. The effects are'#10'random, so pull them at your own peril! And if you''ve ever played a shooter'#10'you know what to expect with barrels: They all explode quite nicely, but'#10'with varying effects depending on the barrel. (Be aware that levers are'#10'activated with the same button as using items -- in this case, open up'#10'your inventory and use the item from there by selecting it and pressing'#10'ENTER.)'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.powerups'; English: 'As you go deeper into the base, you''ll also find some power-ups ("^"). They'#10'are used as soon as you pick them up, and several contain powerful effect,'#10'so use them wisely.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.fire-targeting'; English: 'Oh, I almost forgot! To shoot, just press "f" to fire at the currently '#10'selected target! You can swap between visible targets using "TAB", or '#10'hold "Ctrl" to move the target reticule manually with the arrow keys.'#10'Some weapons allow an alternative fire mode, such as the chainfiring '#10'mode of the chaingun - to use it, press "SHIFT-f".'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.reload-melee'; English: 'Ok, I hear you cry -- "Out of ammo!" Don''t panic. Press "r" to reload your'#10'weapon (Some weapons allow you an alternative reload by pressing SHIFT-r).'#10'Reloading the correct ammo is automatic if you have it in your inventory.'#10'But what if you don''t? You can try pounding on the enemy with your fists'#10'or a hand-to-hand weapon -- to do that just walk into him.  Pick up a'#10'Berserk Pack or a chainsaw (or at least a knife) and you can do some'#10'serious damage that way!'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.dodging'; English: 'Getting hit too frequently when trying to approach an enemy (or when'#10'running from him)? Try not to move in a straight line -- move diagonally,'#10'as this increases your chance to dodge. If you don''t want to approach or'#10'retreat from the enemy, but also don''t want to be hit often, try moving'#10'from side-to-side, perpendicular to the direction of enemy, as this'#10'gives you the same bonus as moving diagonally). Sometimes, in emergencies,'#10'switching to the running tactic and using both of these tricks at once will'#10'give you a much greater chance to dodge than normal.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.prepared-weapon'; English: 'In the equipment menu (remember, press "e") you''ll see that you have a'#10'primary weapon equipped and a secondary weapon at hand.  By pressing "z"'#10'you can switch your primary out for your secondary.  This is faster than'#10'equipping a new weapon from your inventory and it saves you valuable'#10'space.  It''s also quite useful in a firefight, when the situation can'#10'change at any moment!'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.armor-durability'; English: 'Hey, you think you''re cool because you found some armor? Think again. Look,'#10'there are three numbers by the armor. The first two in square brackets are'#10'[current damage resistance/maximum damage resistance]. The second one is '#10'your armor''s health. 100% means it''s undamaged, 1% is almost destroyed.'#10'The more damaged an armor is, the lower its actual resistance is. Keep an'#10'eye on those numbers!'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.damage-resistance'; English: 'Additionally each armor may grant additional resistance to specific damage '#10'types. This information can be obtained in the inventory and equipment '#10'screens.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.player-information'; English: 'Try to check out player information screen by pressing "p" -- some useful'#10'information is written there. First, there are your main parameters:'#10'movement, firing, and reloading speeds, as well as your chance to hit with'#10'the weapon you currently have equipped (from point blank range). Next are'#10'the more passive statistics: your player and dungeon level, amount of'#10'played turns, and your score so far. At the bottom you can find parameters'#10'of equipment that you are currently wearing: damage, firing and reloading'#10'speeds, and accuracy for weapons; and protection value, durability, and'#10'movement and knockback modifiers for armor and boots (yes, armor and boots'#10'can affect your movement speed and chance to be knocked back by powerful'#10'attacks).'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.item-types'; English: 'There are five types of items in this game:'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.common-items'; English: '1) {!Common} - These items are... "common"? You will see them all across the'#10'game, and what you should rely on. Most are generated randomly, but you'#10'will find some are guaranteed in certain places (as a reward for completing'#10'a special level, for example). Common weapons and armor accept 1/1, 3/2,'#10'and 5/3 mods, respectively, depending on the level of your Whizkid trait.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.assemblies'; English: '2) {CAssemblies} - These items can be assembled from other items, usually '#10'common, by modding them with a specific set of mods. Experiment, as'#10'discovered (assembled) assemblies will be recorded in your player data and'#10'can be accessed with the "A" key during the game.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.exotic-items'; English: '3) {VExotic} - These items are rarer and more powerful than common items.'#10'They can potentially appear more than once per game, but are more often'#10'than not found in special levels. Exotic weapons and armor can hold the'#10' same amounts of mods that their common counterparts do.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.unique-items'; English: '4) {GUniques} - Unique items, as the name suggests, can only appear once per'#10'game. Some of them just have cool stats, while others have special'#10'abilities, and each of them come with a unique name. No unique item is'#10'guaranteed, although they can appear in special levels, and they are quite'#10'rare otherwise. Most uniques cannot be modded, but those that do can only'#10'be modded if the player is playing the Technician class.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.artifacts'; English: '5) {YArtifacts} - Artifact items are similar to unique items, except that they'#10'can only appear in very specific special levels, while never appearing'#10'randomly. Finding and collecting these items is almost always a very'#10'challenging task.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.mod-packs'; English: 'Mods (Displayed by a " symbol) are little packs that enhance your weaponry'#10'or armor. Most equipment you find can be modded, so try to cover up their'#10'weaknesses by modding them.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.look-help'; English: 'Whenever you see something that you aren''t familiar with, press "l" to'#10'trigger the Look command -- moving the cursor with the arrow keys will'#10'show what''s under the cursor in the message bar. You can also press "h"'#10'in-game to open up the help menu.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.start.closing'; English: 'Happy fragging!'),
  (ID: 'help.body.keys.title'; English: '{rControls}'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.keys.binding-notice'; English: '  Most keybindings can be redefined via the file Settings menu. The '#10'  following set describes the standard layout, and does not reflect'#10'  any changes you may have made to your keybindings.'),
  (ID: ''; English: ''),
  (ID: 'help.body.keys.navigation'; English: '  {!Escape}      -- Exit menu, or show game menu (Save/Quit/Help/etc)'#10'  {!Arrows}      -- Walking around (PgUp, PgDn, Home, End diagonally!)'#10'  {!Arrows+Ctrl} -- Manually move target reticule'#10'  {!TAB}         -- Switch between targets'),
  (ID: ''; English: '  '),
